e swelling. [Data: Entities (5610, 1895, 5642); Relationships (12518, 12644)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is characterized by thymic predominance, with potential mechanical effects on the airway\",\n      \"explanation\": \"T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (T-LL/T-LBL) is defined by proliferation of immature T-cell lymphoblasts that grow predominantly in the thymus rather than primarily in the bone marrow, making the thymus a common primary site of disease involvement. This provides a clear anatomic signature for the condition within the community. [Data: Entities (2612, 2915, 1221, 3043); Relationships (4786, 5421, 5980)]\\n\\nThe dataset also includes a specific anatomic/clinical consequence: an enlarged thymus can create pressure on the windpipe (trachea). While not explicitly linked to a particular disease in the provided records, this relationship situates thymic enlargement as clinically meaningful due to potential airway compression. [Data: Entities (2388, 1221); Relationships (4813)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Spleen functions and links: lymphocyte production and blood filtration/storage, with oncology relevance noted\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The spleen is described as the largest organ of the lymphatic system and is stated to make lymphocytes, connecting it directly to immune cell biology within this community. This strengthens the network’s repeated theme that lymphatic organs are both structural and functional elements of immunity. [Data: Entities (1154); Relationships (1986)]\\n\\nIn addition to immune function, the spleen is described as playing an important role in blood filtration and storage. This indicates that the spleen’s importance in the network is not limited to lymph/immune roles but also includes hematologic homeostasis. [Data: Entities (1234, 1154); Relationships (1987)]\\n\\nThe spleen description also notes it can be involved through metastatic spread of esophageal cancer, adding an onc